# Embedded external object

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

An embedded object is a file or page from somewhere else, shown inside the page. The HTML elements for this are `<embed>` and `<object>`. A dashboard author cannot write them. Home Assistant’s way to show an external page is the Webpage card, which is the card to use when you need one.

This page explains the two elements so you know why they are not used, and what to do instead.

This page covers:

- Embedded external object
  - `<embed>`
  - `<object>`
    - `<param>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An embedded object is a box, with a default size, that shows content from another source. The source decides what it looks like and how it works. The theme cannot style it. The only part that belongs to the dashboard is the frame around it, which follows `ha-card-border-color` (`#CECECE` in light mode, `#50565A` in dark mode) when the embed is inside a card.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

No Civica theme key applies to an embedded object.

<h3 id="child-embed"><code>&lt;embed&gt;</code></h3>

Shows an outside resource as an interactive area, with a source and a size. It has no fallback content: if the resource cannot be shown, the box is empty. Browser support depends on the kind of file, and the old plugin types are gone. It has no name of its own, so a `title` is needed to say what it holds. A dashboard author cannot write it. Use the Webpage card, or an ordinary image, video or audio card.

<h3 id="child-object"><code>&lt;object&gt;</code></h3>

Also shows an outside resource, but it can hold fallback content that appears when the resource cannot be shown. The fallback must carry the same information (1.1.1 Non-text Content). Give it a `title`. A dashboard author cannot write it.

<h3 id="child-object--param"><code>&lt;param&gt;</code></h3>

Passed named settings to a plugin, inside an object. With plugins gone, it has no use, and a dashboard author cannot write it. It has no accessible meaning of its own.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- An outside page or file can show information you do not have to rebuild.
- The embedded content is outside the theme, so its type, colours and accessibility are the provider’s.
- It may be blocked. A page may refuse to be embedded, a page on plain HTTP cannot appear inside a secure Home Assistant, and a missing plugin shows nothing.
- Embedded content can trap keyboard focus (2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap).
- A blank frame does not mean there is no data.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a Webpage card (`type: iframe`) to show an outside page, and give it a name that says what it shows. Add an ordinary link with visible text as well, so people can open the page when embedding fails, and say that opening it leaves Home Assistant. Describe in words what the embed shows, so the dashboard still makes sense without it.

Keep the card’s security options as they are; the rules and the blocked-frame advice are on [Pictures, rich content and embedded pages](../home-assistant/pictures-and-embedded-content.html#embeds). Do not hide a status behind an embed, and do not put passwords or household details in the address.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: a text equivalent for what the embed shows.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the frame has a title.
- 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap: test that focus can leave the frame.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: test the frame at 320 CSS pixels.
- 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide: embedded moving content may need a control.

See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

